          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Seneca's Early Life</strong><br>\nLucius Annaeus Seneca, known as Seneca the Younger, was born around 4 BCE in the Roman province of Hispania. He was raised in Rome, where he was educated in rhetoric and philosophy. His father, Seneca the Elder, was a famous rhetorical teacher, and his mother's family was influential as well. Despite battling with asthma and other health issues, Seneca emerged as a brilliant student with a keen interest in Stoic philosophy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Seneca's Career and Exile</strong><br>\nSeneca started his career in Rome as a lawyer and a senator. His eloquence and intelligence caught the attention of the ruling emperors. However, his political career had ups and downs, including an exile to Corsica by Emperor Claudius. The charge was an alleged affair with Julia Livilla, the emperor's niece. During his eight years in exile, Seneca wrote many of his works on philosophy, which reflected his Stoic beliefs about virtue and self-control.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Return to Rome</strong><br>\nAfter Agrippina, the wife of Emperor Claudius, secured Seneca's return from exile, he became the tutor to her son Nero. Once Nero became emperor, Seneca was effectively one of the two men who helped govern the Roman Empire. He used his position to influence Nero towards ruling with reason and moderation. However, as Nero's behavior became more erratic, Seneca’s influence waned.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
