          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Seneca's Philosophical Works</strong><br>\nSeneca's philosophical works include dialogues, essays, and letters that explain Stoic philosophy. They are intended to provide practical guidance for living a virtuous life. His works, such as \"On the Shortness of Life\" and \"On Tranquility of Mind,\" offer insights into how to handle adversity and the importance of personal ethics over material wealth or societal status.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Seneca's Tragedies</strong><br>\nApart from philosophy, Seneca was also a significant playwright. His tragedies, such as \"Thyestes\" and \"Phaedra,\" were famous for their intense emotional content and the exploration of darker human themes. They include complex characters and highlight the consequences of vices such as greed, lust, and power. His plays have influenced drama through the centuries, particularly Renaissance and Elizabethan theater.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Seneca and Politics</strong><br>\nIn his role as an advisor to Nero, Seneca tried to curb the young emperor's worst impulses. He wrote speeches and helped manage the administration of the empire. Despite his efforts, Nero grew suspicious of Seneca's power and wealth. This tension eventually led to Seneca's fall from favor as Nero took full control and turned towards tyranny.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
