          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Downfall of Seneca</strong><br>\nAs Nero's reign progressed, Seneca’s relationship with the emperor deteriorated. In 65 CE, Seneca was implicated in the Pisonian conspiracy to assassinate Nero. Although there is no concrete evidence of his involvement, Seneca was found guilty by association. Facing Nero's condemnation, Seneca chose to take his own life, adhering to Stoic principles until the end.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Seneca's Legacy</strong><br>\nSeneca's contributions to philosophy and literature were immense. His philosophical writings became a cornerstone for later Stoic thought, influencing both contemporaries and future generations. His ideas about the ethical use of power, mercy, and personal conduct continue to resonate in modern discussions of philosophy and politics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Later Generations</strong><br>\nLong after his death, Seneca's works have continued to inspire and instruct. His ideas contributed to the development of Christian thought, especially in the understanding of virtue and morality. In literature, his plays have been revisited by writers across different ages, underlining the timelessness of his insights into the human condition. His life and works remain a testament to the enduring relevance of Stoic philosophy.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
